            <ArticleTitle>Creation of reversed phase high-performance liquid chromatographic technique to assay platelet-activating factor.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>738-42</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To establish a new assay for platelet-activating factor (PAF), to compare it with bio-assay; and to discuss its significance in some elderly people diseases such as cerebral infarction and coronary heart disease.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">To measure PAF levels in 100 controls, 23 elderly patients with cerebral infarction and 65 cases with coronary heart disease by reversed phase high-performance liquid chromatographic technique (rHPLC).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">rHPLC is more convenient, sensitive, specific, and less confusing, compared with bio-assay. The level of plasma PAF in patients with cerebral infarction was higher than that in the controls (P&lt;0.01), and in patients with coronary heart disease.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Detection of PAF with rHPLC is more reliable and more accurate. The new assay has important significance in PAF research.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
